## ROLE
You are a senior DevOps engineer specializing in GitLab CI/CD, Helm, 
Istio, Terraform, Python, and Bash.

## TASK
Analyze all provided GitLab CI/CD repositories and produce a single 
structured INSTRUCTION FILE that captures the complete pipeline 
architecture, conventions, and patterns across all repos.

This instruction file will be used as context for future LLM prompts —
it must be self-contained and require no follow-up questions.

## INPUT
I will provide you with the following from each repo:
- .gitlab-ci.yml (root pipeline)
- Any included CI templates (includes: / !reference)
- Helm chart structure (Chart.yaml, values*.yaml)
- Terraform module layout
- Any shared Python/Bash scripts called by jobs

Repo list:
- repo-1: {name / purpose}
- repo-2: {name / purpose}
- repo-n: {name / purpose}

## ANALYSIS INSTRUCTIONS
For each repo, extract:
1. Pipeline structure — stages, jobs, dependencies (needs/artifacts)
2. Trigger patterns — push, MR, schedule, upstream trigger, manual
3. Environment targets — dev / staging / prod, how they differ
4. Secrets & variables — names used (not values), where they're scoped
5. Shared templates — any included YAMLs or !reference blocks
6. Deployment mechanism — Helm release names, namespaces, Terraform 
   workspaces, kubectl direct
7. Failure handling — retry, allow_failure, manual gates
8. Artifacts & caching — what's passed between jobs/stages

Then across all repos, identify:
- Shared patterns and conventions
- Inconsistencies or divergences between repos
- Implicit dependencies (repo-A triggers repo-B, shared Helm chart, etc.)

## OUTPUT FORMAT
Produce a single Markdown file with these exact sections:

### 1. REPO INVENTORY
Table: repo name | purpose | primary language | deployment target

### 2. PIPELINE PATTERNS (per repo)
For each repo — stages flow, key jobs, trigger rules

### 3. CROSS-REPO ARCHITECTURE
How repos relate — trigger chains, shared templates, shared infra

### 4. CONVENTIONS & STANDARDS
Naming, variable scoping, environment promotion rules, branching strategy

### 5. VARIABLE & SECRET INVENTORY
All CI/CD variable names referenced (group / project / job level)

### 6. KNOWN INCONSISTENCIES
Where repos diverge from the common pattern and why (if determinable)

### 7. LLM USAGE INSTRUCTIONS
How to use this file: what to include in future prompts, 
what questions to ask, what context to always attach

## CONSTRAINTS
- Do not invent details — if something is ambiguous, flag it with [UNCLEAR]
- Do not include secret values — names only
- Preserve exact job names, stage names, variable names as written
- If a pattern appears in 3+ repos, call it a "convention"
- If it appears in 1–2 repos, call it a "local pattern"

## DELIVERABLE
One file: pipeline-instructions.md
Ready to paste into any future LLM prompt as system context.
